drumee-roles (1.0.55-1~trixie1) stable; urgency=medium

  * drumee-role-media is now drumee-role-converter. The old name was the name of a
    SOURCE DIRECTORY (server-team/offline/media), and that directory holds two
    unrelated jobs: pure conversion, which needs the file and a toolchain, and content
    operations (add, download, purge, transfer, backfill-posters, serverimport/export)
    which need the database and the MFS model. While the role was defined by the
    directory, distribution.md §2's "runs with no database credentials" was a claim its
    own contents contradicted. Defined by the job — file in, file out — the boundary is
    checkable, and the entrypoint checks it.
  * The dependency set is now measured rather than assumed, counted across server-team
    and the wrapper scripts in /usr/share/drumee/bin: soffice 41, 7z 16, ffmpeg 8,
    pdftotext 4, pdfinfo 3, gm 3, ffprobe 2.
    - ADDED ghostscript, and it is the trap in this role. create-doc-preview.sh
      rasterises the first PDF page with `gm convert -density 200 file.pdf[0]`, which
      GraphicsMagick performs by shelling out to gs. graphicsmagick only RECOMMENDS
      ghostscript and every apt-get in the image builds runs with
      --no-install-recommends; measured on Trixie, that combination fails with
      "Postscript delegate failed" and produces no preview at all. The native channel
      never noticed because it installs the libreoffice METAPACKAGE, which depends on
      ghostscript — so this role's "only the modules we use" optimisation had silently
      removed the delegate.
    - p7zip-full -> 7zip. The former is transitional on Trixie; 7zip owns /usr/bin/7z.
    - DROPPED dcraw: nothing in the platform invokes it, so it was carrying a RAW
      decoder no code path could reach.
    - No ImageMagick and no graphicsmagick-imagemagick-compat: every bare "convert" and
      "mogrify" in the source is "gm convert" / "gm mogrify".
    - No zip: make-zip.sh's $ZIP resolves to 7zz or 7z.
  * docker/Dockerfile.role-converter added, so all seven roles now have an image
    definition. It carries no EXPOSE, deliberately, and creates /data/tmp owned by 8000
    because a fresh named volume inherits the ownership of the directory beneath it.
  * docker/build-role.sh gained --no-cache, and busts the apt layer automatically when
    APT_URI is not apt.drumee.net. Iterating against a local repository means
    re-including a package at the SAME version, which leaves the Dockerfile and every
    build-arg unchanged — so buildx reused the apt layer and the new package never
    reached the image, while the build printed the version it believed it installed.
    Measured: a rebuilt role-converter still carried the previous entrypoint, and the
    check that should have caught it passed against stale content.

 -- Drumee Packaging <packaging@drumee.com>  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 14:23:36 +0200

